Conditionals make code make decisions. JavaScript's `if`/`else if`/`else` is the workhorse:

```js
let score = 85;

if (score >= 90) {
  console.log("A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
  console.log("B");
} else {
  console.log("Keep practicing");
}
```

### Comparison operators
`==` (loose), `===` (strict), `!=`, `!==`, `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`.
Always prefer **`===`** — it checks value *and* type, avoiding surprises:
```js
1 == "1"    // true (loose — converts)
1 === "1"   // false (strict)
```

### Logical operators
```js
let age = 20;
let hasId = true;

if (age >= 18 && hasId) {
  console.log("Allowed");      // AND
}
if (age < 13 || age > 60) {
  console.log("Discount");     // OR
}
if (!hasId) {
  console.log("No ID");        // NOT
}
```

### Truthiness
JavaScript coerces values to booleans in conditions. Falsy values: `false`, `0`, `""`, `null`, `undefined`, `NaN`. Everything else is truthy.
```js
if (name) {
  // runs only when name is not empty/null/undefined
}
```

### Ternary
One-line if/else:
```js
let status = (score >= 50) ? "Pass" : "Fail";
```

### Switch
Clean for matching one value against many constants:
```js
switch (day) {
  case 1: console.log("Mon"); break;
  case 2: console.log("Tue"); break;
  default: console.log("Other");
}
```

